window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"17-28","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"17-28-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-28-01/","title_he":"א – איסור פת","title_en":"The Prohibition of Bread","body_en":"<p>The purpose of the decree prohibiting the bread of gentiles (pat goyim) and the prohibition of food cooked by gentiles (bishulei goyim) is one – to prevent assimilation; however, in halakha there are differences between them. On the one hand, one is more stringent regarding bread, for even if it is of an inferior kind, it is forbidden because of its importance. However, in most of the laws, because of the great need a person has for bread, one is more lenient regarding bread. Therefore, in a time of pressing need, in a place where there are no Jewish bakers, one may be lenient and buy \"pat palter\" (bread of a professional baker) from gentile bakers (as explained in the next halakha).</p><p>The forbidden bread is bread that was baked by a gentile, whether he baked it in his home for his family or baked it in a bakery in order to sell it to the public. Even the inferior bread of the poor, which is never served at a respectable meal, as long as it is made from the five species of grain, has the importance of bread, which is a person's staple food, and the prohibition of gentile bread applies to it (SA 112:1). But breads made from kinds of legumes, such as corn and rice, have the law of a gentile's cooked dish, and therefore if they are fit to be served at a respectable meal, they are forbidden because of bishulei goyim. And if not, they are not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim.</p><p>Baked goods over which one usually recites \"Mezonot\", such as cakes and crackers, since they are defined as bread with regard to the mitzva of separating ḥalla, also have the law of bread, and they are forbidden to eat even when it is not customary to serve them at a respectable meal (above 11:10-12).</p><p>If a Jew participated in baking the bread, the bread is not considered gentile bread and it is permitted to eat it. Therefore, if a Jew lit the oven, or put the dough into it, or stirred the coals, or even only added a small chip of wood to the oven, or even lowered and raised the flame of the gas or the electric oven – the bread is kosher. For the main thing is that the Jewish people know that gentile bread is forbidden to them, and as long as they are careful that a Jew participates in some way in baking the bread, there is already a distinguishing sign for the Jewish people that they must preserve their distinctiveness, and the bread is permitted (Avoda Zara 38b; Rambam; SA 112:9; Levush).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"We learned in Avoda Zara 38b that whenever a Jew performed one of the three tasks of baking bread – lighting the oven, putting the dough into it, or stirring the coals – the bread is kosher. According to Ramban, Rabbeinu Yona and Rosh, stirring the coals renders it kosher because its benefit is noticeable in the bread, but if a Jew stirred in some way whose benefit is not noticeable, the bread is forbidden. On the other hand, according to Rashba, even throwing a small chip of wood into the oven is effective, even though its benefit is not noticeable, and so wrote the Pri Ḥadash and many other Aḥaronim. Similarly, the Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 17:13): \"Even if he only threw a piece of wood into the oven, he has permitted all the bread in it, for the matter is only to serve as a distinguishing sign that their bread is forbidden.\" And the Beit Yosef wrote (112:9) that this is the halakha, since the Rambam and the Rashba agree, and the Rambam gave a reason that is plausible, \"and the custom of Israel, which is Torah, supports them,\" and so ruled the SA 112:9. And the Darkhei Moshe added that so ruled the great Ashkenazi authorities: the Mordekhai, the Maharai, the Issur Ve-heter He-arukh, and the Agur in the name of the Maharil. (The Ran is stringent and holds that bread that belongs to a gentile is forbidden in any case, and when the bread belongs to a Jew, if a Jew performed one of the three acts of baking, the bread is permitted. Most Rishonim and all the Aḥaronim were not concerned for his stringency.)<br>As long as it is possible to improve the bread, if the Jew participates in the heating, even by lowering and raising the flame, he renders the bread kosher (SA 112:12). According to many, even if a Jew turns on the oven by means of a Shabbat timer, it is considered as having been lit by a Jew. So wrote the Minḥat Yitzḥak 4:27 (and Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv was stringent). Likewise, lighting by remote control is effective. It seems that lighting by a minor child is also effective, since there is in this a distinguishing sign that a Jew participated. Admittedly, some hold that only lighting with intention is effective, and not merely throwing a chip of wood into the oven (Shakh 113:10), and then perhaps a minor lacks intention (Bein Yisrael La-amim 4:14); however, the Rema (113:7) wrote that intention is not required. In addition, it seems that the intention of a minor can be effective."}]},{"slug":"17-28-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-28-02/","title_he":"ב – היתר פת פלטר (נחתום)","title_en":"The Permission of Pat Palter (Bread of a Professional Baker)","body_en":"<p>From the time the Sages decreed a prohibition on gentile bread, many Jews found it difficult to keep the decree, since bread is very much needed for sustaining life, and not every person can prepare for himself a large oven for baking bread. And although even a lesser court can annul a decree that did not spread among the Jewish people, the Sages did not annul the decree on bread, since it was accepted with great force, and in practice there were also many Jews who kept it. However, from time to time, when they saw how difficult it was to keep the decree on bread, they said things from which it can be inferred that it would have been fitting to annul it or to restrict it. Thus it is related in the name of Rabbi (the editor of the Mishna) that once, when he was in the field, a gentile brought before him fine bread, and he said: \"How fine this bread is! What did the Sages see to forbid it?\" And another time, when he came to a certain place and saw that bread was scarce for the students, he wondered: \"Is there no palter here?\" A palter is a nahtom, that is, one whose livelihood is baking breads. And they were uncertain whether he meant to say that it would be fitting to permit the bread of a gentile palter in a place where there is no Jewish baker, because with the bread of a palter the concern for becoming close is reduced, since the palter does not give his breads for free as an expression of personal closeness but sells them to the public for payment (Avoda Zara 35b).</p><p>Similarly, it is related (ibid. 37a) about his grandson, Rabbi Yehuda Nesi'a, that after he and his court permitted the decree on oil (above 27:5), he was asked whether he would also permit the decree on bread, for it was known that his opinion inclined that way. He replied that he would not permit bread, because he had already permitted two decrees, and it is not fitting for one court to permit three decrees, for one who permits three decrees is called \"beit dina sharya\" (a permissive court). And thus the decree on bread remained unclear (Yerushalmi Avoda Zara 2:8).</p><p>In practice, in a place where there is no Jewish baker, one may buy bread from a gentile palter, on condition that it is known that all of the bread's ingredients are kosher. But in a place where a Jewish baker is available, as in the State of Israel, there is no permission to buy bread from a gentile baker (SA 112:2).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The Rishonim disagreed regarding the halakha. Some say that in principle the decree on gentile bread is binding, and therefore wherever it is possible to keep the decree, gentile bread is forbidden. However, in a settlement in which there is no Jewish baker, and likewise in the field, it is permitted to eat the bread of a gentile palter. So wrote Ramban, Rashba and Ran, and so they interpreted the Rif. This is also the opinion of the Rambam, as explained in the Baḥ and Arukh Ha-shulḥan 112:5-6. And so it was ruled in SA 112:2; Pri Ḥadash 9, and all who follow their approach. And some say that in a place where a Jewish baker is not available, even the bread of a gentile householder is permitted (Ra'ah, Orḥot Ḥayim). On the other hand, some hold that there were places to which the decree did not spread, and in them there is no prohibition of gentile bread at all; so wrote Tosafot (Avoda Zara 35b, s.v. \"Mi-khlal\"), Rosh, Or Zarua, Semag, and so was the practice in the days of the Rishonim in various communities in Ashkenaz. At the end of the period of the Rishonim, an intermediate ruling was issued in Ashkenaz, that the bread of a gentile palter is permitted even in a place where there is a Jewish baker, whereas the bread of a gentile householder is forbidden (Issur Ve-heter He-arukh 44:1; Rema 112:2; Mateh Yehonatan 4). However, in Ashkenaz too, many ruled that the permission of the bread of a gentile palter is only after the fact (bedi'avad), and when there is a Jewish baker, it is proper to be stringent (Shakh 112:9; Knesset Ha-gedola; Minḥat Ya'akov 75:1; Ḥokhmat Adam 65:2; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 112:17).<br>In practice, one should rule according to the opinion of the SA and most poskim, that only in a place where Jewish bread is not available may one be lenient regarding the bread of a gentile palter. Admittedly, in Ashkenaz there were those who were accustomed to permit this ab initio, and there are those who ruled so in our times as well (Igrot Moshe YD 2:33); however, since among the Ashkenazi poskim too, many inclined to be stringent ab initio, one should rule for members of all communities according to the opinion of the SA and those who agree with him, that the permission applies only in a place where Jewish bread is not available. And it seems that in the Land of Israel, even according to the lenient Ashkenazi poskim who relied on Tosafot and those who agree with them, it will be forbidden to eat the bread of a gentile palter, since they were lenient only in a place where the custom was to be lenient, whereas in the Land of Israel everyone has already been accustomed to be stringent, since it is easy to obtain Jewish bread.<br>All that was permitted regarding a gentile palter applies to bread belonging to gentiles; but with bread belonging to a Jew, for example where the gentile works in a Jewish bakery, if the gentile prepared the bread by himself, it is forbidden (SA 112:11; Pri Ḥadash 7)."}]},{"slug":"17-28-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-28-03/","title_he":"ג – גדרי ההיתר","title_en":"The Parameters of the Permission","body_en":"<p>Outside the Land, in a place where it is difficult to obtain Jewish bread and also difficult to arrange for a Jew to light the baking oven on a regular basis, those who wish to be lenient may buy breads baked by a gentile palter, since with the bread of a palter there is not so much concern for drawing hearts close, as the palter bakes in order to sell to the public. As is the law for bread, so is the law for cakes and all baked goods made from the five species of grain, on condition that all of their ingredients are kosher.</p><p>If the palter baked breads for his household – they are forbidden. If an ordinary person baked breads in order to sell them to the public – they are permitted, for everything follows the intention of the baking (Rashba; Beit Yosef; Rema 112:2; SA 7; Shakh 11).</p><p>A place in which, because of pressing need, it was permitted to eat the bread of a gentile palter, and a Jewish baker arrived there – the permission is annulled. But if the Jewish baker cannot supply all the needs of the community, or the gentile's breads are better, it is permitted to continue buying from the gentile. Even when the Jewish baker's breads are good, but he does not have all the kinds of bread and baked goods, it is permitted to buy what he does not have from the gentile baker (SA 112:4-5; Knesset Ha-gedola). However, when in general the Jewish bakers supply all the needs of the community, even though in certain respects the gentiles' baked goods are better or have a certain distinctiveness, one should not be lenient and eat from them, since there is no need for it, only indulgence. Therefore, throughout the Land of Israel, it is forbidden to eat the bread and baked goods of gentiles, since Jewish kinds of breads and baked goods are available in abundance.</p><p>The permission to eat the bread and baked goods of a gentile palter is on condition that all the ingredients from which they were prepared are kosher. In the past, people generally knew from what ingredients each baked good was prepared, and therefore every Jew could know whether the baked good that the gentile palter had prepared was made from kosher ingredients. However, today, bread and other baked goods are composed of dozens of ingredients intended to improve the taste, enhance the rising, beautify the baked good, extend its shelf life and the like. These ingredients may be made from the fats of pigs or of calves that were not slaughtered according to halakha, or may have been produced from insects (below 37:1-5). Therefore, the permission to eat the bread of a gentile palter outside the Land today is on condition that it was checked and found that all the ingredients from which he prepared his baked goods are kosher. Since a private individual generally does not know how to check this, in a place where there is a need to permit the bread and baked goods of a gentile palter, rabbis from the area must check this and, to the extent possible, rule that it is permitted.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-28-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-28-04/","title_he":"ד – מנהגי המחמירים ודין היוצאים לחוץ לארץ","title_en":"The Customs of Those Who Are Stringent, and the Law for Those Traveling Abroad","body_en":"<p>Even outside the Land, in places where most of the public was accustomed to be lenient and eat the bread of a gentile palter, there were many who were meticulous and were stringent with themselves not to eat the bread of a gentile palter. However, for a great or important need, such as for a Shabbat meal, even the meticulous were accustomed to be lenient and eat the bread of a gentile palter.</p><p>When one who is meticulous not to eat the bread of a gentile palter hosts at his table a guest who is not meticulous about this, in a place where the custom is that the host recites the blessing over the bread and breaks it for the guest as well, as long as the gentile's bread is finer than the Jew's bread, out of respect for the guest and the mitzva of hospitality, he should break the gentile's bread, and since he has already begun eating from it after the blessing, he is permitted to eat from it throughout the meal (SA 112:13). But in a place where the custom is that each person recites the blessing for himself, as many are accustomed today, the host should recite the blessing over Jewish bread, and let the guest recite the blessing over the fine gentile bread (MB 168:21).</p><p>If one who is careful about gentile bread dines together with friends who are not careful, as long as their gentile bread is finer, he is permitted to eat gentile bread with them, so that he will not appear to be holding himself above them with his stringency and harm the good relations that should prevail between them (Rema 112:15; Shakh 21).</p><p>When a person who is meticulous not to eat gentile bread did not manage to obtain Jewish bread for himself, if he can obtain Jewish bread by walking or traveling for 18 minutes (the measure of walking a mil), he should take the trouble to bring it. If it is farther away, he is permitted to eat gentile bread. And if he is on the road, if he knows that within 72 minutes further along his way he will be able to obtain Jewish bread, he should wait until he buys Jewish bread. If he would have to wait longer, he may eat gentile bread (SA 112:16; Pitḥei Teshuva 6; Peninei Halakha: Tefilla 2:4, n. 4).</p><p>As is the law for those who are stringent outside the Land, so is the law for Israelis who travel abroad: they are considered like those who are stringent about gentile bread, and in a time of need they are permitted to be lenient.<sup>3</sup></p><p>The poskim recommended that even those who are lenient about gentile bread should conduct themselves like those who are stringent at least during the Ten Days of Repentance (SA OḤ 603:1).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"In cases in which even the stringent were accustomed to be lenient, such as for the needs of Shabbat, they were lenient even regarding the bread of a gentile householder, for in a place where there was no bread of a gentile palter, they were lenient regarding the bread of a gentile householder (SA 112:8). However, nowadays there is no need for this leniency, since the bread of a palter is available, whereas regarding the bread of a gentile householder there are more halakhic doubts, on account of the ingredients he mixed into the bread and on account of the oven, which is presumed to be non-kosher (treif)."}]},{"slug":"17-28-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-28-05/","title_he":"ה – בישולי גויים","title_en":"Bishulei Goyim (Food Cooked by Gentiles)","body_en":"<p>An additional safeguard that the Sages established for the Jewish people to keep them from assimilating among the gentiles is the prohibition of bishulei goyim (above 27:6). That is, even when there is no kashrut problem at all with the foods or with the pots in which they were cooked, it is forbidden for a Jew to eat dishes that a gentile cooked. Included in the prohibition of cooking are all foods prepared for eating by means of the heat of fire or electricity, including those fried in deep oil in a pot or in a little oil in a frying pan, roasted over fire, or baked or toasted in an oven. Likewise foods cooked by steam or in a microwave or in any manner in which it is customary to use heat to cook. But foods prepared by gentiles by salting, pickling or cold smoking are permitted (SA 113:13).<sup>4</sup></p><p>The prohibition of bishulei goyim applies to dishes that have a certain importance, which a person can serve at a respectable meal, and consequently they may lead to drawing hearts close. But with simple foods, there is not so much concern for drawing close, since the cooking was not significant for them, and therefore they are not subject to the prohibition. The Sages stated two rules in defining important dishes: the first, that they are not eaten raw as they are, but rather it is the cooking that makes them fit for eating. For example, milk and its products are eaten raw, and therefore they are not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim, whereas meat and flour are not eaten raw, and therefore their cooking is subject to the prohibition. The second rule, that they are served at a king's table to accompany bread; but if they are dishes that only simple people are accustomed to eat, they are not subject to the prohibition. The first rule is the main one in practice, because it is the one that determines the law of most foods, whereas the second rule pertains to individual foods, because today, thanks to variety and openness, almost all kinds of food are served at a king's table.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Simply understood, every customary method of heating is included in the prohibition of bishulei goyim. Admittedly, the Ben Ish Ḥai (Year Two, Ḥukat 22) was lenient regarding steam, since it was not included in the decree, and so wrote Yabi'a Omer vol. 5, YD 9. However, it seems that the decree applies to any method customary for preparing food with heat. Or in other words, any method whose result is similar to the result of cooking with heat; and so wrote Rav Halperin (Kashrut Ve-Shabbat Ba-mitbaḥ Ha-moderni, p. 174). And so wrote regarding steam: Levushei Mordekhai YD Telita'i 10; Shevet Ha-levi vol. 6, 108:6. And so wrote regarding a microwave: Shevet Ha-levi 8:185; Shevut Yitzḥak vol. 6, p. 61, in the name of Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv; Bein Yisrael La-amim 7:4-5. Foods prepared by cold smoking are not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim.<br>The prohibition applies when the gentile intended to cook; but if he lit an oven in order to heat the house, and there was a dish there that became cooked, the dish is permitted (Avoda Zara 38a; SA 113:5; and see Pri Ḥadash 11)."},{"n":5,"en":"In the Gemara (Avoda Zara 38a) it was said that in Sura they learned in the name of Rav: \"Whatever is eaten raw as it is – is not subject to bishulei goyim,\" and in Pumbedita they learned: \"Whatever is not eaten at a king's table to accompany bread – is not subject to bishulei goyim.\" Some say that the main ruling is as they said in Sura (Ramban, Ra'avya and Eshkol), and one opinion holds that the halakha follows both versions stringently, that is, only what is eaten raw and is not served at a king's table is permitted (Agudah in the name of Rabbeinu Ḥananel). And according to most poskim, the halakha follows both versions leniently (Rambam, Rabbeinu Tam, Semag, Sha'arei Dura, Or Zarua and many others, and so it was ruled in SA 113:1).<br>The poskim wrote regarding both rules that one follows the custom of the place, and one does not take into account the customs of individuals, even if they are groups (Maharikash; Pri Ḥadash 113:3; Shiyurei Berakha 1; Beit Yehuda YD 21; Ḥokhmat Adam 66:4; and others). Today the geographical place is not the determining definition for a person's perception, for people move from place to place, and in every place live people of different cultures; therefore we must define the concept of place as the \"environment\" in which the person lives, as will be explained in the next two halakhot. The environment includes family members, friends and neighbors with whom one has contact. That is, the Sages forbade dishes that have importance, and importance for each person is determined according to the environment familiar to him, and not according to what he heard that there are people who do so in various places."}]},{"slug":"17-28-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-28-06/","title_he":"ו – הכלל הראשון – אינם נאכלים חיים","title_en":"The First Rule – They Are Not Eaten Raw","body_en":"<p>The first rule in the prohibition of bishulei goyim is that it concerns a food that people are not accustomed to eat raw. That is, a food that cooking makes fit for eating, and consequently the cooking is significant for it. Therefore, there is a prohibition on dishes of meat and fish, since they are not eaten raw. Likewise there is a prohibition on dishes of grain and flour, such as groats, ptitim and noodles. And likewise on dishes of legumes, such as lentils, chickpeas and rice. And even a food that can be eaten raw in a time of pressing need, such as an egg, which can be swallowed raw, is subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim (Avoda Zara 38b; SA 113:14-15).</p><p>Milk and all its products, such as cheese and butter, are not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim, since they are eaten raw without cooking. And although today it is customary to heat milk for pasteurization (to disinfect it of bacteria), since the intention of pasteurization is not to improve its taste, the milk is considered as eaten raw.</p><p>Fruit is not subject to the prohibition, since it is customary to eat it raw. Therefore, fruit jam is not subject to the prohibition. Likewise, spices are not subject to the prohibition, since it is customary to mix them into foods that are eaten raw as well, such as salad (MB 203:11).</p><p>Most vegetables are not subject to the prohibition, since they are eaten raw. Therefore, matbuḥa salad is not subject to the prohibition, since its ingredients, which are tomatoes, peppers and onions, are eaten raw. But there are vegetables that in many places people are not accustomed to eat raw, such as potatoes, zucchini, beets and eggplants, and consequently they are subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim. However, one in whose environment people eat these vegetables raw as well, for example by mixing slices of them into a salad, is not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim with them. And even though he and his family members are not accustomed to eat them raw, since he knows that many in his environment sometimes eat them raw, for him too they are considered foods that are eaten raw. But if he only heard that there are some who eat them raw, they are subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim for him.</p><p>Determining the status of a food, whether it is eaten raw, depends on its state before cooking. For example, in the past people were accustomed to eat raw wheat kernels, and therefore if they were cooked – they were not subject to the prohibition. But if the wheat was ground into flour, since flour is not eaten raw, every dish made from it was forbidden. And today people are not accustomed to eat raw wheat kernels, and they too are subject to the prohibition. Fish and meat that were salted until they became fit for eating, if afterwards a gentile cooked them, they are not subject to the prohibition, since already before the cooking they were fit for eating (SA 113:12).<sup>6</sup></p><p>If things that are eaten raw, such as cheese and vegetables, and things that are not eaten raw, such as flour and egg, were mixed in one dish, one follows the main ingredient, according to which the blessing is also determined (SA 113:2).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"The source of the law of wheat kernels and flour is Tashbetz 3:11; Erekh Ha-shulḥan 113:11; Zivḥei Tzedek 41. There are foods, such as couscous and bulgur, that undergo cooking in the factory and are afterwards dried with heat until they are not fit for eating, and at home one must pour boiling water over them, and thereby they become fit for eating. Some say that as long as it is possible to prepare them in a kli sheni (a secondary vessel), they are not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim (Divrei David vol. 3, YD 17). And some say that since their proper preparation is with boiling water, they are subject to the prohibition of cooking. And so it seems. Patties prepared in a Jewish factory, such as corn and soy patties, since heating is sufficient for them, if a gentile heated them in an oven, they are not subject to the prohibition. Sugar is not subject to the prohibition, since it is not eaten by itself (Tuv Ta'am Va-da'at, second edition, 225).<br>Roasted sesame and tehina made from roasted sesame are not subject to the prohibition, because it is customary to eat raw sesame as well and to make tehina from raw sesame as well.<br>Fruit is not subject to the prohibition of cooking. Admittedly, some are stringent regarding unripe fruit, because it could not have been eaten raw (Kitzur SA 38:11). However, since for a Jew who eats it there is no advantage in its having been made from unripe fruit, one follows the general rule for the species, which is not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim (Issur Ve-heter He-arukh 43:4; Gilyon Maharsha). There are rare fruits that are not eaten without cooking, such as quinces, and they are subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim.<br>Although many today are accustomed to sometimes eat raw fish in various forms, as in sushi, and likewise some are accustomed to eat raw meat ground or thinly sliced (carpaccio), meat and fish are subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim, since the form in which they are eaten raw is completely different and is considered another type of eating, and therefore they should not be regarded as sometimes eaten raw. Moreover, it is impossible that because of one form of eating the prohibition of cooking should be annulled for meat and fish, for a considerable part of the work of cooking is intended for meat and fish, and the most important cooked and roasted foods are of meat and fish. However, with sushi itself, even though the rice is cooked, there is no prohibition of bishulei goyim, since the main ingredient is the fish, which is not cooked."}]},{"slug":"17-28-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-28-07/","title_he":"ז – הכלל השני – עולה על שולחן מלכים","title_en":"The Second Rule – Served at a King's Table","body_en":"<p>The second rule is that the food \"is served at a king's table to accompany bread.\" \"A king's table\" means a meal of distinguished people, and \"to accompany bread\" means that it is eaten as part of a meal, either as the main part of the meal in order to be satiated, or as a tasty food that it is customary to serve as dessert at the end of the meal. That is, even a dish of a food that is not eaten raw, if it is not customary to eat it as part of a respectable meal, is not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim. And everything is according to time and place. Therefore, in our times, as a result of the enormous variety of foods and the openness to different kinds of cuisine, all the foods that people are accustomed to serve at a meal can be served at a king's table. And even a person who is not accustomed to serve a particular dish at a respectable meal, as long as he knows that in his environment it is sometimes customary to serve it, it is subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim for him.</p><p>However, foods that are not intended for eating or for dessert as part of a meal, such as candies, chocolate, kinds of roasted seeds and nuts (pitzuḥim) and other snacks, are not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim.</p><p>Breakfast cereals and rice cakes are subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim, since it is customary to eat them to satiety at breakfast.</p><p>Coffee and alcoholic beverages are not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim, since they are intended for drinking and they consist mainly of water, which people are accustomed to drink raw. The proof is that over coffee one recites \"Shehakol\" and not \"Ha-etz,\" the blessing over coffee beans, and over vodka and beer one recites \"Shehakol\" and not \"Ha-adama,\" the blessing over the grain kernels from which they are prepared (Tosafot Avoda Zara 31b, s.v. \"Ve-tarvayhu\"; Taz 114:1). And some added another reason for permitting it, that coffee and tea are not necessarily served as part of the meal (Pri Ḥadash 6; Zera Emet OḤ 41).<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"The poskim wrote that \"served at a king's table\" does not mean kings specifically, but ministers and dignitaries are also included (Issur Ve-heter He-arukh 43:2; Ḥayim She'al vol. 1, 74:5; Ben Ish Ḥai, Year Two, Ḥukat 9). In the past, when society was more divided into classes, there were dishes of the common people, such as small fish and porridge, such that one who served them before ministers was considered to be insulting their honor (Avoda Zara 38a). However, today, thanks to variety and openness, all the foods that people are accustomed to cook are considered respectable, and ministers too are accustomed to eat them at a meal. Moreover, unlike the past, most ministers grew up in ordinary homes, and they like what they ate in their parents' home and with their friends.<br>Some understood that the meaning of \"served at a king's table\" is that it is one of the important and respectable dishes of the meal, for which a person invites his friend (see Or Ha-mizraḥ 36, p. 169; Bishulei Akum 4:2). However, many disagreed with this and included in this any dish that is served at a respectable meal. In addition, it is difficult to determine which dish is considered respectable, since the customs of people and communities differ from one another. And some ruled that any dish served in a hotel or at a wedding is considered respectable, and whatever is not served is not respectable. However, one who looks will find that in practice, in hotels and weddings all kinds of foods are served according to the wishes of the guests.<br>There is an additional component in this condition, that \"bread is accompanied by it.\" Some say that this refers to dishes that are eaten with bread (Maharitatz, Knesset Ha-gedola). However, according to most poskim it refers to any dish that is eaten for satiety, that is, that it helps the bread to reach the same goal of satiety (Rashba, Meiri, Pri Ḥadash and others). And even breakfast cereals, which are intended for satiety at breakfast, are included in this.<br>Some say that the dessert served at the end of the meal is not considered \"accompanying bread\" and is not subject to the prohibition (Pri Ḥadash 113:3; Tiferet Le-Yisrael). But according to most poskim it is included in \"accompanying bread,\" since it is eaten as part of a meal of satiety; and so wrote Ritva, Tur, SA 113:1, Pri To'ar, Panim Me'irot, Ḥokhmat Adam, Arukh Ha-shulḥan 6-7.<br>But kinds of roasted seeds and nuts (pitzuḥim) are not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim, since their main purpose is casual eating between meals, and a person does not extend a respectable invitation to his friend in order to eat them (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 17:17; Rema 113:2). And consequently, so is the law for candies, chocolate and snacks. And although they are sometimes brought at the end of meals as well, they are not considered foods that it is customary to bring at meals."}]},{"slug":"17-28-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-28-08/","title_he":"ח – השתתפות ישראל מתירה","title_en":"Participation of a Jew Permits","body_en":"<p>When a Jew is a partner in the cooking, and even if he helped it only slightly, for example by improving it through stirring its contents while it was cooking, the dish is kosher. All the more so the dish is kosher if the Jew placed it on the fire, or lit the fire under it, or raised it in a manner that helps the cooking (SA 113:6-7).<sup>8</sup></p><p>Regarding bread we learned (in halakha 1) that even participation in lighting the fire before the baking begins is effective in rendering kosher the bread that was afterwards baked by a gentile; however, regarding cooking there is a disagreement. Some are lenient and hold that the law of a cooked dish is like the law of bread, and whenever a Jew added a chip of wood to the fire on which the dish will afterwards be cooked, or slightly lowered the gas flame and raised it again, since he was a partner in kindling the fire, all the dishes that the gentile will afterwards cook on that fire will be kosher. And this is the custom of those of Ashkenazi descent.</p><p>And some are stringent and hold that only regarding bread were they lenient in this, since it is very much needed by a person, but regarding a cooked dish, only the participation of a Jew at the time of the cooking itself, by placing the dish on the fire, or by adding fire under it in a manner that helps it, renders it kosher. And this is the custom of those of Sephardic descent (SA 113:7).</p><p>For example, when frying an omelet or meat in a frying pan, according to the lenient opinion a Jew may light the fire under the frying pan, and afterwards a gentile may fry on it many omelets or pieces of meat for hours. And according to the stringent opinion, throughout the entire frying it must be a Jew who places the omelets or pieces of meat on the frying pan, because lighting the fire under the frying pan is effective only for the food that is on it at that time.</p><p>Since today members of all communities live together, and members of the Eastern and North African communities were accustomed to be stringent, even though the law is rabbinic, ab initio one should rule for members of all communities according to the stringent opinion. And after the fact, since the law is rabbinic, one should rule for members of all communities according to the permissive opinion. And in places where there is no personal closeness between the cook and the diners, such as in a public kitchen or in a restaurant where the cook does not go out to the diners, one may ab initio grant regular kashrut certification according to the lenient opinion, but \"mehadrin\" certification may be granted only to those who follow the stringent opinion.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"The Jew's contribution to the cooking must continue at least until the stage at which the food is fit for eating in a time of pressing need, like ma'akhal ben Drosai (food cooked to the degree that the bandit Ben Drosai would eat it), and afterwards, even if the gentile removes the dish from the fire and returns to finish its cooking, the dish is kosher. But if before the dish became fit for eating in a time of pressing need, the Jew's effect was interrupted, for example the fire went out, and a gentile lit a fire and placed the dish on it, the dish is forbidden, because the Jew was not a partner in bringing the dish from a state in which it is not fit for eating to a state in which it is fit for eating in a time of pressing need (SA 113:8).<br>Some say that if the gentile cooked it to the degree of ma'akhal ben Drosai, it will no longer help for a Jew to continue cooking it, since it has already become ready for eating (Rabbeinu Ḥananel, Rashba, Ran and Rivash; and see Pri Ḥadash 113:18). And some say that as long as its cooking has not been completely finished, if a Jew participates in its cooking, it is permitted (Rosh, Semag, Rabbeinu Peretz, Ra'ah, Ritva, Issur Ve-heter He-arukh). In SA 113:9 he ruled ab initio according to the stringent opinion, and on Erev Shabbat or in a case of great loss according to the lenient opinion, and the Rema was lenient ab initio.<br>If a gentile lit the fire and a Jew placed a large piece of meat on it, if over time it will all become roasted, even if the gentile turned the piece of meat over and hastened and improved its roasting, all the meat is kosher, since even without his help the meat would have become fit for eating in a time of pressing need. But if through the Jew's placing of the meat only one side would have become fit for eating in a time of pressing need, the side that was roasted without any help from the Jew is forbidden (Ramban, Rashba, SA 113:10, Rema according to Ḥokhmat Adam, and others)."},{"n":9,"en":"Those who are stringent and hold that lighting the fire before the cooking begins is not effective are: Rabbeinu Yona, Rashba, Ra'ah, Ran, Rivash and SA 113:7. And the lenient ones are: Ra'avan, Rabbeinu Peretz, Or Zarua, Mordekhai, Kol Bo, Maharai, Issur Ve-heter He-arukh and Rema there. Some hold that after the fact, according to the lenient approach, a Jew may light a candle, and afterwards the gentile may light from it the fire under the dish, but ab initio one should permit only when a Jew at least throws a chip of wood into the fire on which the gentile is about to cook (Issur Ve-heter He-arukh 43:13; Rema). On the other hand, some are stringent and hold that even according to the lenient approach, with bishulei goyim throwing a chip of wood is not effective, but only when a Jew contributes significantly to the fire on which the gentile will cook (Maharshal; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 113:44).<br>According to the stringent approach, when the gentile cooks for the public and has no connection with the diners, in a time of need one may be lenient (Yabi'a Omer vol. 9, YD 6), and some are stringent (Or Le-Tziyon vol. 2, p. 12).<br>Some say that according to the approach of the SA, when meat is roasted on a round rotating device (shawarma), even if a Jew turned on the heating elements after the meat was placed there, it is forbidden for the gentile to slice the meat, because when slicing he enables the inner layer to be cooked; it turns out that by the act of slicing the meat he cooks the inner meat (Divrei David vol. 4, YD 13). However, it seems more likely that since even without the slicing, over time all the meat would have become fit for eating in a time of pressing need, the meat is kosher according to all opinions. And one should add the opinion of the lenient ones, that even by a Jew's lighting the fire before the meat was placed there, all the meat that is afterwards roasted there will be kosher."}]},{"slug":"17-28-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-28-09/","title_he":"ט – בישול עסקי וציבורי, ושכירים בבית ישראל","title_en":"Commercial and Public Cooking, and Hired Workers in a Jewish Home","body_en":"<p>Some say that just as they were lenient in a time of need to buy bread from a gentile palter, since there is not so much drawing close with baked goods baked for many people, so too one should be lenient with a gentile who cooks for the public, such as a cook in a restaurant who has no connection with the diners, that in a time of need his dishes are permitted (Maharitatz). However, according to most poskim, the permission of \"pat palter\" is specific to bread, on which life depends, but regarding cooked foods the Sages made no distinction in their decree, and the prohibition applies whether the cooking is done in a home kitchen or in a commercial or public kitchen. However, in a time of need, when there are additional reasons to be lenient, one may rely on the lenient opinion in this.</p><p>One opinion holds that the prohibition applies when the gentile cooks in his own home, but if he cooks in a Jewish home, there is no prohibition. In practice the poskim ruled that the prohibition applies even when the gentile cooks in a Jewish home.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"In Responsa Maharit Tzahalon (Yeshanot 161), he compared bishulei goyim to gentile bread: just as in a time of need they permitted the bread of a gentile palter, so in a time of need one may eat dishes that a gentile cooked for the public for payment or in order to sell them. And so wrote the Paḥad Yitzḥak and the Yishrei Lev (by the author of the Ḥikrei Lev). On the other hand, many did not mention this leniency, and some wrote explicitly to forbid it, among them: Tashbetz 1:89; Shiyurei Berakha 112:9; Erekh Ha-shulḥan 3; Sdei Ḥemed. And this is the accepted ruling. In a time of need one adds the approach of the Maharitatz in order to be lenient. However, when the gentile cooking is done in an industrial factory, where the distance between the cook and the buyers is much greater, the Igrot Moshe wrote (vol. 4, YD 48:5) that many are accustomed to be lenient and one should not protest against them.<br>It is cited in Tosafot (Avoda Zara 38a, s.v. \"Ela\") that according to Rabbi Avraham, the prohibition applies to a dish that the gentile cooks in his own home, but a dish that he cooks in a Jewish home is not forbidden, since there is no concern for intermarriage and no concern of stumbling by eating forbidden food. Rabbeinu Tam did not agree with his opinion, and so wrote the Kol Bo, Ra'avya, Meiri, Agudah, and so it was ruled in SA 113:1.<br>Some of the Rishonim permitted eating the cooking of male and female slaves owned by a Jew, for since they cook under compulsion, eating their dishes does not involve drawing close (Rabbi Yitzḥak son of Rabbi Manoaḥ). And the Ramban in his responsa (those attributed to the Ramban, 284) was lenient regarding a slave who underwent a slave's conversion, and with this it is reasonable that all would agree. And some learned from the Ramban to be lenient even regarding slaves who did not undergo conversion. And so wrote the Ra'ah (Bedek Ha-bayit 3:7), and after the fact he agreed to be lenient in this. And the Issur Ve-heter He-arukh 43:13 and the Rema permitted the cooking of slaves in a Jewish home. And the Rashba (Responsa 1:68), Ra'avan and Ritva were stringent even after the fact regarding a slave who did not undergo conversion. And to this the SA 113:4 inclined. Regarding hired workers, the Aḥaronim wrote that even the lenient ones would be stringent, since they are not subordinate like male and female slaves (Baḥ, Knesset Ha-gedola, Pri Ḥadash). However, it is possible that the basis for the permission regarding slaves exists to a certain extent also with hired workers who must obey their employers, and in a time of need one may add this reasoning toward leniency (see Shakh 113:7; Sdei Ḥemed, Ma'arekhet Bishulei Akum 1; Minḥat Yitzḥak 7:62).<br>In the IDF, according to army orders, only a Jewish cook lights the fire, and even if it went out only a Jew relights it, and only a Jew places foods on the fire. However, in my humble opinion, in a time of pressing need, in a kitchen that is not properly organized, one may rely on the lenient approach, since the cooking is for the public, and it is in a Jewish home (the IDF), and he does it by order of the commander (similar to compulsion), and there is great pressing need in this, for estrangement from a gentile who enlisted in the IDF is something that may harm the cohesion of the soldiers."}]},{"slug":"17-28-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-28-10/","title_he":"י – חולים וסיעודיים","title_en":"The Sick and Those Requiring Nursing Care","body_en":"<p>A sick person who needs a cooked dish on Shabbat, even though his illness is not dangerous, it is permitted to ask a gentile to cook a dish for him. This is because the prohibition of asking a gentile to perform melakha on Shabbat is rabbinic, and for a sick person the Sages permitted their prohibitions (Peninei Halakha: Shabbat 28:2). Included in the permission to ask a gentile to cook on Shabbat for a sick person, they also permitted the sick person to eat from the gentile's dish (Ran and Levush).</p><p>If some of the dish remained for Motza'ei Shabbat, some say that even healthy people are permitted to eat from it, since it was cooked with permission for the sick person (Ra'ah and Rema 113:16). And some say that on Motza'ei Shabbat even the sick person himself is forbidden to eat from the dish that the gentile cooked for him, since now a Jew can cook the foods he needs (Rashba and Ran). And this is the opinion of most Aḥaronim (Taz 15, Pri Ḥadash, Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav, Pri To'ar, Ḥokhmat Adam, Ben Ish Ḥai, Year Two, Ḥukat 25).</p><p>An elderly or sick person who needs a gentile caregiver to look after him, as long as the person being cared for is able to light the fire on which the gentile will cook, or the oven in which the gentile will bake or roast, he must exert himself and take the trouble to do so. And if he cannot, it is a mitzva for his children and family members to exert themselves as much as they can to prepare dishes for him and place them in his refrigerator, and the gentile will heat them for him. In a time of pressing need, when the elderly or sick person has no relatives or friends who can bring him dishes cooked by Jews, since he is sick and greatly needs this, he may rely on the opinion of the few who are lenient in this. But his family members and visitors are forbidden to eat from those dishes.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The permission for a sick person who is not in danger to eat from dishes cooked by gentiles is unique to Shabbat; and so wrote the Rashba (Mishmeret Ha-bayit 3:7), Levush 328:19; Pri Megadim, Mishbetzot Zahav 6. For since they permitted for a sick person the rabbinic prohibition (shevut) of instructing a gentile on Shabbat, they also permitted dishes cooked by gentiles; but on a weekday they did not permit it at all, since there are Jews who can cook for him (unlike the Minḥat Yitzḥak 3:72). However, if the sick person or the elderly person in need of nursing care has no children or relatives to cook for him, since he is in a situation of very great pressing need, he may be lenient and eat from the dishes of the gentile man or woman who cares for him, similar to the leniency of asking a gentile to cook on Shabbat for the sick person. And it is perhaps possible to add the approach of Rabbi Avraham in Tosafot, who permitted eating from a dish of a gentile who cooked in a Jewish home. However, it seems that his permission applies when there is a Jewish householder there, and the gentile is subordinate to him and cooks under his instructions, and therefore there is not so much drawing close from eating his dishes, which are considered the dishes of the household. But here, the caregiver becomes like the householder, and the concern for drawing close increases. If possible, it is preferable that a Jew light a candle, and from it the gentile should light the fire every time he cooks, for some are lenient in this after the fact, as explained in note 9. Likewise, if the family members are able to cook the important dishes, it is proper that in a time of pressing need they be lenient to have the gentile cook only simple dishes, such as porridge and an egg (if possible, from that same candle), for some are lenient and hold that they are not included in the prohibition, because in their view the prohibition applies only to important dishes for which guests are invited, as cited above in note 7. In any case, family members who are guests of the elderly person in need of nursing care are forbidden to eat from the dishes, since their situation is not one of pressing need, as they can cook for themselves.<br>When the gentile cooked a dish for a Jew on Shabbat, I wrote according to the opinion of most poskim, who forbade it on Motza'ei Shabbat. Seemingly, if the gentile cooked in a Jewish home, it should have been permitted because of a double doubt (sefek sefeka) in a rabbinic prohibition, as wrote Halikhot Olam vol. 7, Ḥukat 10, and Tzitz Eliezer 21:61: perhaps the halakha follows those who permit it on Motza'ei Shabbat, and perhaps the halakha follows Rabbi Avraham, who permits a dish that a gentile cooked in a Jewish home. However, as I wrote, when the permanent caregiver cooks, it is difficult to add the reasoning of Rabbi Avraham. In addition, in our times, when it is possible to keep cooked food refrigerated and afterwards heat it on the hot plate, the sick person's relatives must arrange for the preparation of dishes for the sick person before Shabbat, and if they did not take the trouble to do so and were forced to ask a gentile to cook for the sick person on Shabbat, there is no room to permit benefiting from it on Motza'ei Shabbat."}]},{"slug":"17-28-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-28-11/","title_he":"יא – דין הכלים","title_en":"The Law of the Utensils","body_en":"<p>Utensils in which a gentile cooked dishes, even though all the ingredients of the dish were kosher – the utensils require kashering, for whatever the Sages forbade, they forbade similar to a Torah prohibition, and just as utensils in which Torah prohibitions were cooked require kashering, so utensils in which rabbinic prohibitions were cooked require kashering (Rashba and Ran). And some say that the utensils do not require kashering, since the basis of the prohibition of bishulei goyim is lest they come to drawing close and intermarriage, and from the taste absorbed in and adhering to the utensils there is no concern for drawing close (Rosh and Ra'ah).</p><p>In practice the halakha follows the stringent opinion, and the utensil in which the gentile cooked requires kashering by hagala (purging in boiling water) or libun (heating with fire). But after the fact, if they transgressed and cooked in the utensil without kashering, the dish is permitted. However, the utensil is still forbidden for use, since it has not yet been kashered.<sup>12</sup></p><p>If the utensil in which the gentile cooked was earthenware, it is kashered by hagala three times. And although if other forbidden foods were cooked in it, according to the overwhelming majority of poskim an earthenware utensil may not be kashered by hagala, regarding the prohibition of bishulei goyim, whose basis is rabbinic, one relies on those who hold that an earthenware utensil can be kashered by hagala three times (SA 113:16; see above 32, n. 4).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"The Rosh and Ra'ah did not require kashering, for in their view the taste absorbed in the utensils will not be more important than a dish that is not served at a king's table, which involves no concern for intermarriage and no prohibition of bishulei goyim. And the Rashba and Ran required kashering the utensils, because the absorbed taste follows the dish, and since the dish is forbidden, consequently the taste that was absorbed from it in the pot is also forbidden. In SA 113:16 he ruled without attribution according to the stringent opinion, and mentioned the lenient ones as \"some say.\" And although Rabbi David Pardo and the Minḥat Kohen were lenient like the Rosh and Ra'ah, most Aḥaronim ruled stringently, among them: Mateh Yehonatan, Pri To'ar, Ḥokhmat Adam, Arukh Ha-shulḥan.<br>However, a utensil in which a gentile cooked for a sick person on Shabbat, according to most poskim does not require kashering, since he cooked for him with permission (Erekh Ha-shulḥan 113:24; Zivḥei Tzedek 65; Gedulot Elisha; unlike the Ben Ish Ḥai, who was stringent).<br>When they cooked in utensils without kashering, the utensils still require kashering, but the dish is permitted, even if the cooking was in an earthenware or wooden utensil, since according to most poskim bishulei goyim are nullified in a majority. So wrote Ramban, Nimukei Yosef, Issur Ve-heter He-arukh, Rema 112:14; Shakh 113:21; Birkei Yosef 10. Admittedly, according to the Be'er Sheva and the Minḥat Kohen, the prohibition of bishulei goyim is nullified only in sixty; however, the halakha follows the lenient opinion, both because they are the majority and because the dispute concerns a rabbinic law."}]}]});
